Chapter: CapabilityDoors
  cites Foreword chapter Capability

 A binary reaches the kernel through two doors, and the chapter under test
 exists to make both doors grant it the same authority. The boot grant
 expands a capability by NAME: boot-cap-mask-for calls cap-bits-for-name.
 The verified loader expands one by ID: cdx-cap-to-kern-bits calls
 cap-bits-for-id, and the id it holds is whatever the manifest stored,
 which is cap-id-for-name. So a real binary travels name, then id, then
 bits, and the claim is that it arrives where the boot path arrives in one
 step. That claim was written down and never executed.

 Nothing had ever called any of this. Foreword chapter Capability was
 cited by exactly one test, the compile smoke test, so all twenty-four
 names resolved, every function type-checked, and none was asked an
 answer.

 An agreement count on its own is satisfied by two doors that both answer
 zero, which is the shape this project is repeatedly caught by, so the
 count of rows granting a NON-ZERO mask is reported beside it. A table
 that expanded to no authority at all would agree perfectly and show up
 here as zero.

 The three masks printed at the end are the re-checkable part. They are
 readable straight off the table: Console holds base bit 0 with read 4 and
 write 5, so read is 1 plus 16, write is 1 plus 32, and readwrite is 1
 plus 16 plus 32. FileSystem has no base bit and read 6 and write 7, so it
 is 64, 128, and their union. Concurrent has no direction and carries ipc
 and process-create as extra bits, so all three directions answer alike.

 We say:

Section: Both doors, every row, every direction

  doors-agree : List CapSpec, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  doors-agree (ts) (i) (len) (dir) (acc) =
    if i >= len then acc
    else let s = list-at ts i
    in let by-name = cap-bits-for-name (s.cs-name) dir
    in let by-id = cap-bits-for-id (cap-id-for-name (s.cs-name)) dir
    in doors-agree ts (i + 1) len dir (acc + (if by-name == by-id then 1 else 0))

  doors-nonzero : List CapSpec, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  doors-nonzero (ts) (i) (len) (dir) (acc) =
    if i >= len then acc
    else let s = list-at ts i
    in let by-name = cap-bits-for-name (s.cs-name) dir
    in doors-nonzero ts (i + 1) len dir (acc + (if by-name /= 0 then 1 else 0))

  test-doors : Text =
    let ts = capability-table
    in let n = list-length ts
    in let ar = doors-agree ts 0 n cap-dir-read 0
    in let aw = doors-agree ts 0 n cap-dir-write 0
    in let arw = doors-agree ts 0 n cap-dir-readwrite 0
    in "agree=" & show (ar + aw + arw) & "/" & show (n * 3)

  test-nonzero : Text =
    let ts = capability-table
    in let n = list-length ts
    in let zr = doors-nonzero ts 0 n cap-dir-read 0
    in let zw = doors-nonzero ts 0 n cap-dir-write 0
    in let zrw = doors-nonzero ts 0 n cap-dir-readwrite 0
    in "granting=" & show (zr + zw + zrw) & "/" & show (n * 3)

Section: A direction is a refinement, never a third thing

 For a family that has a direction, asking for read and asking for write
 must together be exactly what asking for both gives, and neither alone
 may be all of it. For a family that has no direction the answer cannot
 depend on the direction at all. cap-dir-bits ends in an unguarded
 readwrite branch, so a direction constant that drifted would not fail a
 comparison, it would fall through and hand out both bits.

  dir-union-ok : List CapSpec, Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  dir-union-ok (ts) (i) (len) (acc) =
    if i >= len then acc
    else let s = list-at ts i
    in let r = cap-bits-for-name (s.cs-name) cap-dir-read
    in let w = cap-bits-for-name (s.cs-name) cap-dir-write
    in let rw = cap-bits-for-name (s.cs-name) cap-dir-readwrite
    in dir-union-ok ts (i + 1) len (acc + (if bit-or r w == rw then 1 else 0))

  cd-both : Boolean, Boolean -> Boolean
  cd-both (a) (b) = if a then b else False

  dir-distinct-ok : List CapSpec, Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  dir-distinct-ok (ts) (i) (len) (acc) =
    if i >= len then acc
    else let s = list-at ts i
    in let r = cap-bits-for-name (s.cs-name) cap-dir-read
    in let w = cap-bits-for-name (s.cs-name) cap-dir-write
    in let rw = cap-bits-for-name (s.cs-name) cap-dir-readwrite
    in let directional = s.cs-read-bit >= 0
    in let ok = if directional then cd-both (r /= rw) (w /= rw) else cd-both (r == rw) (w == rw)
    in dir-distinct-ok ts (i + 1) len (acc + (if ok then 1 else 0))

  test-directions : Text =
    let ts = capability-table
    in let n = list-length ts
    in "union=" & show (dir-union-ok ts 0 n 0) & "/" & show n & " refines=" & show (dir-distinct-ok ts 0 n 0) & "/" & show n

Section: A capability that was not granted must not verify

 A miss answers negative one for an identifier and zero for a mask. Zero
 is the honest answer for an unknown capability: no authority. An unknown
 name and an unknown id are both asked, because the loader is reached by
 id and a manifest carrying a number nobody assigned is exactly the case
 that must not open a door.

  test-ungranted : Text =
    let bad-id = cap-id-for-name "NotACapability"
    in let r = cap-bits-for-name "NotACapability" cap-dir-read
    in let w = cap-bits-for-name "NotACapability" cap-dir-write
    in let rw = cap-bits-for-name "NotACapability" cap-dir-readwrite
    in let by-id = cap-bits-for-id 999 cap-dir-readwrite
    in "unknown-id=" & show bad-id & " unknown-name-bits=" & show (r + w + rw) & " unknown-id-bits=" & show by-id

Section: The vocabulary is derived, not stored

 Eighteen rows give twenty-four names, because Console, FileSystem and
 Network each admit a dotted refinement in an effect row. A dotted name is
 a direction on a capability and not a capability of its own, so exactly
 the eighteen undotted names may resolve to a row and the six dotted ones
 must not. That is the derivation stated as an arithmetic anyone can
 re-check, and it needs no string search to say it.

  vocab-resolving : List Text, Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  vocab-resolving (ns) (i) (len) (acc) =
    if i >= len then acc
    else vocab-resolving ns (i + 1) len (acc + (if cap-id-for-name (list-at ns i) >= 0 then 1 else 0))

  test-vocab : Text =
    let ns = capability-names
    in let n = list-length ns
    in let rows = list-length capability-table
    in "names=" & show n & " rows=" & show rows & " resolving=" & show (vocab-resolving ns 0 n 0)

Section: The masks themselves

  test-masks : Text =
    let cr = cap-bits-for-name "Console" cap-dir-read
    in let cw = cap-bits-for-name "Console" cap-dir-write
    in let crw = cap-bits-for-name "Console" cap-dir-readwrite
    in "console=" & show cr & "," & show cw & "," & show crw

  test-masks-fs : Text =
    let fr = cap-bits-for-name "FileSystem" cap-dir-read
    in let fw = cap-bits-for-name "FileSystem" cap-dir-write
    in let frw = cap-bits-for-name "FileSystem" cap-dir-readwrite
    in "filesystem=" & show fr & "," & show fw & "," & show frw

  test-masks-conc : Text =
    let kr = cap-bits-for-name "Concurrent" cap-dir-read
    in let krw = cap-bits-for-name "Concurrent" cap-dir-readwrite
    in "concurrent=" & show kr & "," & show krw

Section: Body

  opening : [Console] Nothing = act
    print-line-uni test-doors
    print-line-uni test-nonzero
    print-line-uni test-directions
    print-line-uni test-ungranted
    print-line-uni test-vocab
    print-line-uni test-masks
    print-line-uni test-masks-fs
    print-line-uni test-masks-conc
  end
